---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
export function getStaticPaths(){return projects.map((project)=>({params:{slug:project.slug},props:{project}}));}
const {project}=Astro.props;
const injaz=project.slug==="injaz";
const life=project.slug==="life-advise";
---
<SiteLayout title={`${project.name} — Osama Ibrahim`} description={project.description}>
<section class="page-hero"><div class="container"><a class="read-link" href="/projects/">← All projects</a><small class="eyebrow"> / {project.category}</small><h1>{project.name}</h1><p>{project.description}</p><div class="case-meta"><span>{project.status}</span>{project.stack.map((item)=><span>{item}</span>)}</div><img class="case-image" src={project.image} alt={`${project.name} project screenshot`}/></div></section>
<section class="section"><article class="container simple-content">
{injaz ? <><small class="eyebrow">PRODUCT CASE STUDY / IN PROGRESS</small><h2>Bringing organizational work into one clear system.</h2><p>INJAZ is an organizational management platform being developed to help teams structure everyday work, responsibilities and operational records. Its product direction is built around clarity, accountable workflows and practical administration.</p><h3>What is being built</h3><p>Current product work includes role-aware access, employee and department administration, operational reporting and claims workflows. The platform remains in development; this page does not claim a public production release.</p><h3>Engineering focus</h3><p>The implementation uses a web application architecture with Supabase and PostgreSQL at its data layer. Requirements, access boundaries and end-to-end scenarios are being validated before release.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>In development. A public demo will be added only after a verified deployment is approved for public viewing.</p></div></> : life ? <><small class="eyebrow">PROJECT CASE STUDY / RECOVERY</small><h2>An offline-first experience for thoughtful advice.</h2><p>Life-Advise was created as a quotes and advice application designed to remain useful when connectivity is limited. The original project description highlights offline-first behavior and guidance around life, success and money.</p><h3>What the project explored</h3><p>The project is a useful record of progressive web app thinking: designing for unreliable connections, making content available locally and keeping the experience lightweight.</p><h3>Current state</h3><p>This is historical work currently being reviewed for recovery. Its former public URL and source repository are retained as references, but current operational status has not been independently verified.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>Historical project · recovery in progress. No current live-demo claim is made.</p></div></> : <><small class="eyebrow">PROJECT NOTES / HISTORICAL WORK</small><h2>A practical build, and a lesson carried forward.</h2><p>{project.description}</p><h3>What is documented</h3><p>The original portfolio records this project and its associated technology notes. Links are retained as historical references; current availability, source integrity and runtime compatibility have not yet been re-checked.</p><div class="evidence"><b>Evidence status</b><p>Historical project. Current deployment and compatibility remain to be verified.</p></div></>}
<div class="case-links">{project.source && <a class="read-link" href={project.source} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Source repository ↗</a>}{project.reference && <a class="read-link" href={project.reference} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Previously listed link ↗</a>}{project.live && <a class="read-link" href={project.live} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Previously listed URL ↗</a>}</div>
</article></section>
</SiteLayout>
